160	}
161	//! `paste_attributes` — Edit ▸ Paste Attributes and Edit ▸ Paste Graphic
162	//! Properties (spec §6, table "Clips"; spec §11 G-32).
163	//!
164	//! Its own module because it is the clipboard's OTHER use: `paste` makes a
165	//! payload fit and places it, and this copies properties onto clips that
166	//! are already there and places nothing. The two share one thing, the
167	//! payload, whose shape stays `edit_paste.rs`'s to know and is read through
168	//! its [`parse_payload`]. Cut out of `edit_paste.rs` under the size rule
169	//! (core/CLAUDE.md), as a pure move, ahead of G-32.
170	//!
171	//! `paste_attributes` copies the EARLIEST payload clip's property groups
172	//! onto clips already on the timeline. It is not in `edit_props.rs` because
173	//! the material comes from the clipboard, not from a caller's patch. A
174	//! group is REPLACED whole, never merged — "make this look like that" is
175	//! the gesture, and a merge would leave the target's shadow under a source
176	//! that had none. What a target keeps is what makes it the clip it is
177	//! rather than how it looks: a text clip's `content` and the `words` that
178	//! time it (spec §11 W-6 — a timing belongs to its words); a shape's
179	//! `kind`, `width`, `height`, `from` and `to`. Video and audio travel whole,
180	//! transform included — `audio.muted` and `keep_pitch` are properties like
181	//! opacity, and `keep_pitch` only acts when the speed is not 1, which does
182	//! not travel: `speed`, `enabled`, the actions, the transition, the name,
183	//! the label and the source are not properties at all.
184	//!
185	//! **The qualifier follows the clip kind** (spec §11 GR-13): only an
186	//! adjustment clip takes one, so a picture target gets none (dropped, which
187	//! `paste_attributes`' description says — D2-1: a paste's answer is not
188	//! the place for it), and an adjustment target keeps its own when the
189	//! payload clip is a picture clip, which has none to give. Between two
190	//! adjustment clips it is copied like any other video key.
191	//!
192	//! ### The fifth group
193	//!
194	//! `graphic` (G-32) copies a graphic clip's `props`, whole, onto clips
195	//! drawn by the SAME module, and the target keeps its `name`, `width`,
196	//! `height`, `in` and `length`. It is a group a caller NAMES and never one
197	//! of the default set: props hold a graphic's words as well as its look,
198	//! and ⌥⌘V has no picker. That is why the names are [`PasteGroup`] and not
199	//! `patch::Group`: a patch has no `graphic` group (G-10), and `Group` is
200	//! matched exhaustively wherever a patch is read.
201	//!
202	//! The copied map is checked for SHAPE only — `model_graphic.rs`'s
203	//! `check_props` with no schema, then `check_count` — and only when a
204	//! target takes it, the first time one does: a paste in which no target
205	//! takes `graphic` never reads the map, as the four groups are checked
206	//! only after they have landed on a target. The write is `insert` and
207	//! `shift_remove`, `model_graphic.rs`'s rule, so a key both sides have
208	//! keeps its slot. A target key the payload lacks that an action on the
209	//! target names (`actions_graphic.rs`'s `names_prop`) is KEPT in its slot,
210	//! so the action's ramp keeps its start (spec §11 G-34 (d), amending
211	//! G-32 (e)).
