329	impl Project {
330	    /// Inserts a clipboard payload's clips with fresh ids, the earliest one
331	    /// landing exactly at `at` and the rest keeping their offsets from it
332	    /// (spec §6).
333	    ///
334	    /// `track` names where the payload's front-most depth lands and retargets
335	    /// the rest by the same index delta, the way `move_clip` does; absent, it
336	    /// is the front-most track, so a paste keeps the copy's own layering.
337	
338	| What's wrong | What to do |
339	| --- | --- |
340	| The point is on a clip, not in a gap | Move the playhead into the gap, or check the current track. |
341	| No clip follows the gap on that track | There's nothing to pull back. |
342	| The track is locked | [Unlock it](/timeline/hide-and-lock-tracks/). Its clips are the ones that would stay behind. |
343	| Closing it would push clips into an overlap the overlap rule forbids, on any track that moves | Trim or move those clips first. |
344	| A linked clip would go before the start of the timeline | Happens when a sound leads its picture, as in a [J-cut](/audio/j-and-l-cuts/). Move the pair later, or trim the sound. |
345	
346	## Insert time
347	
348	Inserting time opens empty room. Every clip on an unlocked track that starts at or after a point moves later by a length, and so does every marker at or after that point, unlike a ripple.
349	
350	Clips that span the point stay put, so nothing is cut. To open room inside a clip, [split](/editing/splitting/) it first.
351	
352	Inserting time has no menu item or gesture of its own. An ⌥-drop [insert](/editing/insert-and-overwrite/) and [Freeze Frame](/speed/freeze-frame/) both do it as part of their edit, and an agent can do it directly with `insert_time`. See [Tools: cutting and deleting](/mcp/tools-cut-and-delete/).
353	
354	## Open room by hand
355	
356	<Steps>
357	
358	1. Move the playhead to where the room should start.
359	
360	2. Press <kbd>⌥⌘A</kbd> (**Edit ▸ Select After Playhead**) to select every clip that starts at or after the playhead.
361	
362	3. Drag the selected clips later, or press <kbd>⇧.</kbd> to [nudge](/editing/nudge-clips/) them 10 frames at a time.
363	
364	</Steps>
365	
366	This differs from inserting time in two ways: it also moves the selected clips on locked tracks, and it leaves markers where they are. Start the drag on a clip on an unlocked track, because a clip on a locked track can't be grabbed.
367	
368	## What a copy keeps
369	
370	A copy is the whole clip: its source, trim, speed, name, label, picture and sound settings, and actions. From then on it's a clip of its own, so changing one doesn't change the other.
371	
372	- **Linked clips.** If a clip has a [detached sound](/audio/detach-and-attach/), its partner is copied too and the two copies are linked to each other. A partner on a locked track isn't copied.
373	- **Nest clips.** A copy of a nest clip made with Duplicate or ⌥-drag shows the same nest as the original, so an edit inside one shows in both. See [Shared and independent nest copies](/editing/nest-copies/).
374	
375	## When Duplicate won't work
376	
377	Copies follow the same overlap rule as any other placement, and Frame Job doesn't push later clips out of the way.
378	
379	- A copy may overlap the clip after it by up to half of the shorter clip. The overlap gets a Cross Dissolve, as any new overlap does. See [Transitions](/motion/transitions/).
380	- A longer overlap stops the duplicate. Frame Job beeps, nothing changes, and the reason appears at the foot of the inspector. See [When an edit is refused](/getting-started/refused-edits/).
381	- An adjustment clip can't overlap anything, so a copy of one needs clear space.
382	
383	To duplicate into a tight edit, open room first or use **Edit ▸ Copy** and **Edit ▸ Paste**. A paste that has no room goes on a new track.
384	
385	## Related
386	
387	- [Copy, cut and paste](/editing/copy-and-paste/)
388	- [Move clips](/editing/move-clips/)
389	## Where pasted clips land
390	
391	Paste keeps the arrangement you copied.
392	
393	- **In time.** The earliest clip lands at the playhead, and every other clip keeps its distance from it. Everything lands on a frame.
394	- **Across tracks.** Each clip keeps its place in the stack, counted back from the front. A clip copied from the front track lands on the front track, and one copied from the second track down lands on the second track down. A selection across two lanes pastes back onto two lanes.
395	- **Missing tracks.** If the project has too few tracks, new ones are added at the back.
396	
397	## When there's no room
398	
399	A paste isn't turned down for lack of room. A clip that would break the overlap rule on its track goes on a new track at the front, so it stays visible over what was already there.
400	
401	A smaller overlap is allowed, as it is for any placement: up to half of the shorter clip. The clip stays on its track and the overlap gets a Cross Dissolve. See [Transitions](/motion/transitions/).
402	
403	## Paste into another project
404	
405	Copy in one project, switch to another and paste. The clips stay on the clipboard, so the other project can be opened before or after you copy.
406	
407	- **Media.** Files are matched by their path. A file the project already has is reused, and one it doesn't have is added to the [Media tab](/projects/media-tab/). Only files the pasted clips use come across, including the `.cube` file of a [look](/color/luts/) and the LUT of [Camera Log](/hdr/camera-log/).
408	- **Frame rate.** Pasted clips are snapped to this project's frame rate.
409	- **New identities.** Pasted clips are new clips, so they can be pasted again into the project they came from.
410	
411	A graphic clip brings its name and not its files. In a project without that graphic, the clip draws a red error card until you add a graphic of the same name. See [Insert a graphic](/graphics/insert-a-graphic/).
412	
413	## A detached sound comes along
414	
415	When a clip has a [detached sound](/audio/detach-and-attach/), selecting its picture copies both, so the pair pastes as a linked pair. Selecting the sound on its own copies only the sound, and it pastes as an ordinary clip with no link to any picture.
416	
417	Cut follows the same split. Cutting a picture takes its sound with it, unless the sound is on a locked track. Cutting a detached sound on its own leaves the picture in place, silent.
418	
419	## Copied clips as text
420	
421	Paste into a text editor and you get a one-line summary of what was copied, in the project's timecode:
422	
423	```text
424	Frame Job — 2 clips, 00:00:01:00 to 00:00:04:00
425	```
426	
427	## Good to know
428	
429	:::caution
430	**Nest clips can't be pasted.** The clipboard holds the nest clip but not what's inside it, so **Paste** shows an error and pastes nothing, even if only one of the copied clips is a nest clip. **Cut** still deletes a nest clip, so press <kbd>⌘Z</kbd> to bring it back. To make another copy of a nest clip, use [Duplicate or ⌥-drag](/editing/duplicate-clips/).
431	:::
432	
433	- A paste lands whole or not at all. If one clip can't go in, Frame Job shows an error naming it and nothing changes. An [adjustment clip](/color/adjustment-clips/), for example, can't be pasted while a [nest](/editing/nests/) is open.
434	- Room for a paste isn't unlimited: a project holds up to 64 tracks and 4,096 clips. A paste that would pass either limit shows an error.
435	- Copy and Paste work inside a nest. **Copy** takes the clips you picked and counts their depth against the nest's own tracks, and **Paste** lands in the nest that's open.
436	
437	## With an agent
438	
439	`paste` takes a description of the clips in the same form Copy writes, so an agent can place clips without touching your clipboard, at any time and on any track. See [Tools: copy and nest](/mcp/tools-copy-and-nest/).
440	
441	## When it is available
442	
443	Freeze Frame works on a selected clip under the playhead. With nothing selected there, it's dimmed. A playhead exactly at a clip's end isn't on that clip. With several selected clips under the playhead, it acts on the first.
444	
445	It needs a clip of a video file with its picture. An audio-only clip, text, a shape, a picture, a graphic, an adjustment clip, a nest or another still can't be frozen. The command stays available on those, and Frame Job shows an error saying the clip isn't a media clip or has no video properties. Nothing changes.
446	
447	## Everything stays in sync
448	
449	The 2 s opens across every unlocked track, so the cut stays together:
450	
451	- Clips that start at or after the playhead move 2 s later, on every unlocked track.
452	- Markers at or after the playhead move with them.
453	- A clip on another track that crosses the playhead is neither cut nor moved. It plays on through the hold, as a music bed would.
454	- Sound [linked](/audio/detach-and-attach/) to the frozen clip is cut at the playhead too, and its second half moves with the picture.
455	- Clips on locked tracks stay where they are.
456	
457	## Good to know
458	
459	Frame Job won't freeze where the still would overlap another clip by too much. It beeps, the foot of the inspector says why, and nothing changes. Two places do this:
460	
461	- Inside a [transition](/motion/transitions/), where the two clips already overlap.
462	- On a [locked track](/timeline/hide-and-lock-tracks/), because the rest of that track can't move out of the still's way.
463	
464	## With an agent
465	
466	`freeze_frame` can hold any moment of a clip without moving the playhead, for any length of at least one frame. `slip` on a freeze frame moves the frame it holds. See [Tools: speed and time](/mcp/tools-speed/).
467	
468	## Related
469	## What a speed change does
470	
471	- **The clip's start stays and its length changes.** A faster clip can leave a gap after it, and later clips don't move up to fill it. [Close Gap](/editing/close-gaps/) closes it.
472	- **The new length snaps to a whole frame**, and never runs past the end of the file at the new rate. See [The new length](/speed/fit-to-duration/#the-new-length).
473	- **Actions keep their places** as fractions of the clip's length.
474	- **On a ramped clip** the sheet sets the base rate and scales the ramp with it. See [Edit a ramped clip](/speed/edit-a-ramped-clip/).
475	
476	A speed ramp works the other way round: it leaves the clip where it is and changes how much of the source plays.
477	
478	## A slowed clip overlapping the next
479	
480	A slower clip gets longer, so it can run into the clip after it. Later clips stay where they are, so the two now overlap.
481	
482	- **If the overlap is allowed**, the later clip gets a transition across it, and the readout names that transition.
483	- **If it isn't allowed**, the slowed clip keeps its old speed. Any other selected clips still change.
484	
485	An overlap is allowed up to half the length of the shorter clip. It isn't allowed when one of the clips is an adjustment clip, or when the clip would reach a second neighbour on the same side. See [Transitions](/motion/transitions/).
486	
487	## Good to know
488	
489	- **Clips at different rates.** The field opens on ×1, and **Apply** sets every listed clip to the rate in the field, even if you only changed **Preserve pitch**. Choose **Cancel** to leave them at their own rates.
490	- **Preserve pitch** applies to every listed clip, and changing it alone counts as a change.
491	- **Linked clips.** Frame Job won't change the speed of a clip linked to its detached sound until you attach it. See [Linked clips](/speed/overview/#linked-clips).
492	- **Inside a nest**, the sheet works on the clips you've selected in the open nest.
493	
494	## With an agent
495	
496	`set_speed` does the same job, one clip at a time. It can also take a length in place of a rate, which is [Fit to](/speed/fit-to-duration/). An agent isn't asked about ×12: its answer says which clips lost their sound. Its undo step is named **Speed**. See [Tools: speed and time](/mcp/tools-speed/).
497	
498	## Which edges and clips stretch
499	
500	| You drag with ⌥ | What happens |
501	| --- | --- |
502	| A media clip's end edge | A rate stretch |
503	| A clip's start edge | An ordinary trim |
504	| The end edge of a still, text, shape, graphic or any other clip | An ordinary trim, with the undo step **Trim Clip** |
505	| An edge on a locked track | Nothing. The edge doesn't move. |
506	| The end edge of a clip linked to its detached sound | Nothing, until you [attach it](/audio/detach-and-attach/) |
507	
508	## Where a stretch stops
509	
510	The clip stops following the drag when the next step would break a limit, and it lands at the last length that worked:
511	
512	- **Rate limits.** The stretch can't need a rate outside ×0.1 – ×1000.
513	- **One frame.** The clip can't get shorter than one frame.
514	- **The next clip.** A stretch into the next clip follows the same [overlap rule](/speed/change-speed/#a-slowed-clip-overlapping-the-next) as any slowed clip, so the later clip gains a transition across an allowed overlap.
515	
516	Drag back inside the limit and the clip follows you again.
517	
518	## A ramped clip
519	
520	On a clip with a speed ramp, the whole ramp stretches with the clip and keeps its shape. The stretch stops where any of its rates would pass the limits. See [Edit a ramped clip](/speed/edit-a-ramped-clip/).
521	
522	## Stretching past ×12
523	
524	If a stretch takes a clip with sound past ×12, Frame Job puts the stretch back and asks first.
525	
526	- **Set Speed** applies the stretch you made.
527	- **Cancel** leaves the clip as it was.
528	
529	See [Sound at high speed](/speed/sound-at-high-speed/).
530	## Nests inside nests
531	
532	A nest can hold other nests, up to eight levels deep. Select a nest clip with other clips and press <kbd>⌘G</kbd>, or press it while a nest is open.
533	
534	The depth counts the whole project, even while you edit inside a nest, so an edit that looks fine there can still stop at the limit. The same goes for the project's limits of 64 tracks and 4,096 clips, and a nest's own tracks count towards the 64.
535	
536	## When Nest won't work
537	
538	**Clip ▸ Nest** is greyed out with nothing selected, and when the only selected clip is already a nest clip, since wrapping one nest in another on its own changes nothing you can see.
539	
540	Two things stop a nest. Frame Job beeps, changes nothing, and gives the reason at the foot of the inspector.
541	
542	- **Something unselected is in the way.** The nest clip would overlap a clip you didn't select on its track, by more than the overlap rule allows. This often means an unselected clip sits inside the span. Select it too, or move it.
543	- **The selection holds an adjustment clip.** An [adjustment clip](/color/adjustment-clips/) can't go inside a nest.
544	
545	If the nest clip overlaps the clip before it by an amount the rule allows, it gets a Cross Dissolve over the overlap. See [Transitions](/motion/transitions/).
546	
547	## With an agent
548	
549	`nest` can name the nest as it's made, and `get_project` lists every nest with its own tracks and clips. See [Tools: copy and nest](/mcp/tools-copy-and-nest/).
550	
551	## Related
552	## When a paste stops
553	
554	Some picture settings can't land on some kinds of clip. When one can't, Frame Job shows an error naming the clip and changes nothing, even on the other selected clips.
555	
556	| The target | Can't take |
557	| --- | --- |
558	| An adjustment clip | Blur, sharpen or pixelate filters, a blend mode, Remove Background, Text Behind Subject, motion blur |
559	| A nest clip | A blend mode, Remove Background, Text Behind Subject, motion blur |
560	| A text, shape or graphic clip | Remove Background |
561	| A movie or still clip | Remove Background with its subject set to Foreground, which only an image clip can take |
562	| Any clip | A crop that would leave nothing of it, such as one copied from a much larger source |
563	
564	To get past one, take the setting off the copied clip, copy it again and paste, or select only the clips that can take it.
565	
566	## With an agent
567	
568	An agent can do two things the menu item can't. It can name which groups travel, so it can paste the sound alone and leave the picture settings. And with groups named, it can paste across a mixed selection without stopping, skipping any clip that can't take a group. See [Tools: copy and nest](/mcp/tools-copy-and-nest/).
569	
570	## Related
571	## When it can't run
572	
573	Frame Job beeps and gives the reason at the foot of the inspector:
574	
575	| What happened | What to do |
576	| --- | --- |
577	| A file is missing or can't be read | [Relink it](/projects/relink-media/), then choose the command again. |
578	| The analysis of a file failed | The message names the file and the reason. Nothing is split. |
579	| The analysis is no longer in the project's cache | Choose the command again, and Frame Job reads the file again. |
580	
581	## With an agent
582	
583	`split_at_shots` works on the clips it's given. Without a list, it takes every clip of a video file on an unlocked track, so a clip on a locked track is cut only when it's named. It shows no sheet. It waits up to 60 seconds for the cuts, and if they aren't ready it stops without splitting and keeps what was found, so calling it again carries on.
584	
585	`get_media_analysis` reads a file's cuts without splitting anything. See [Tools: cutting and deleting](/mcp/tools-cut-and-delete/).
586	
587	## Related
588	## Good to know
589	
590	### Still images
591	
592	A picture on the timeline looks the same backwards, so it has no Reverse rows and nothing to render. The same goes for a [freeze frame](/speed/freeze-frame/).
593	
594	### Linked clips
595	
596	Frame Job won't reverse a clip linked to its detached sound. The foot of the inspector says which clips are linked. Choose **Clip ▸ Attach Audio** (<kbd>⇧⌥⌘D</kbd>) first. See [Detach and Attach Audio](/audio/detach-and-attach/). With several clips selected, the others still reverse.
597	
598	### Commands that need a forward clip
599	
600	- [Generate Captions](/captions/what-can-be-transcribed/) skips a reversed clip, because its words would come out backwards.
601	- [Auto Zoom](/recording/auto-zoom/), [Add Shortcut Overlays](/recording/shortcut-overlays/) and [Speed Up Typing](/recording/speed-up-typing/) won't work on one.
602	
603	## With an agent
604	
605	`set_reversed` says which way to play. It isn't a toggle. A project an agent has open with no window shows no sheet and starts no render, so its reversed clips stay on the slate until the project is opened in a window. See [Tools: speed and time](/mcp/tools-speed/).
606	
607	## Related
608	## When Apply is unavailable
609	
610	Nothing changes in any of these cases. The readout gives the reason, and it can include clip and action numbers and times in ticks, at 600,000 ticks to the second. The checks run in this order, so fixing one can show the next.
611	
612	| The problem | What to do |
613	| --- | --- |
614	| The span overlaps an action already on the clip. This counts any action, including one that would sit inside a dip's or burst's hold. The reason names it, and it's never replaced. | Set an in and out range clear of the action, or [move or remove](/speed/build-a-ramp/#move-and-resize-an-action) it. |
615	| A **Speed Dip** or **Speed Burst** span is shorter than two frames. | Widen the in and out range. |
616	| The rate is on the wrong side of the starting rate, such as a **Ramp Up** to a rate that isn't faster. | Change **Rate** or **Shape**. The reason gives the starting rate. |
617	| The clip is linked to its detached sound. | Choose [Attach Audio](/audio/detach-and-attach/) first. |
618	| The ramp would [play past the end of the file](/speed/speed-ramps/#never-past-the-end-of-the-file). | Use the largest rate that fits, which the reason names, or trim the clip's end. |
619	
620	## Good to know
621	
622	- Taking a clip with sound past ×12 asks once first, on the sheet. **Set Speed** closes the sheet and applies the ramp, and **Cancel** leaves the sheet open. See [Sound at high speed](/speed/sound-at-high-speed/).
623	- Captions on a Captions track are re-timed in the same step. See [Captions follow a speed change](/speed/captions-and-speed/).
624	
625	## With an agent
626	
627	`add_speed_ramp` isn't tied to the in and out range. It takes its span directly, and with no span it covers the whole clip. It can also use any action curve and any hold from 0 to 90%. A span running past the clip's end isn't accepted, which can't happen from the sheet. See [Tools: speed and time](/mcp/tools-speed/).
628	
629	## Related
630	## With an agent
631	
632	`retime_range` makes the same edit, but it takes the range as times, so it needs no In and Out. It never asks about ×12: the clip's audio is left out, and the answer says so. See [Tools: speed and time](/mcp/tools-speed/).
